## Run a Test Job

With the resource manager enabled for production usage, users should now be able
to run jobs. To demonstrate this, we will add a "test" user on the **head** node
that can be used to run an example job.

OpenHPC includes a simple "hello-world" MPI application in the
`/opt/ohpc/pub/examples` directory that can be used for this quick compilation
and execution. OpenHPC also provides a companion job-launch utility named `prun`
that is installed in concert with the pre-packaged MPI toolchains. This
convenience script provides a mechanism to abstract job launch across different
resource managers and MPI stacks such that a single launch command can be used
for parallel job launch in a variety of OpenHPC environments. It also provides a
centralizing mechanism for administrators to customize desired environment
settings for their users.

## Interactive Execution

To use the newly created "test" account to compile and execute the application
**interactively** through the resource manager, execute the following (note the
use of `prun` for parallel job launch which summarizes the underlying native job
launch mechanism being used). Under Flux, `prun` launches one MPI task per
allocated node unless the number of tasks is set via `FLUX_JOB_NTASKS`:

```bash
# Switch to "test" user
su - test

# Compile MPI "hello world" example
[test@sms ~]$ mpicc -O3 /opt/ohpc/pub/examples/mpi/hello.c

# Submit interactive job request and use prun to launch executable
[test@sms ~]$ flux alloc -N 2 -n 8

[test@c1 ~]$ FLUX_JOB_NTASKS=8 prun ./a.out

[prun] Master compute host = c1
[prun] Resource manager = flux
[prun] Launch cmd = flux run -N 2 -n 8 -o pmi=pmix ./a.out (family={{ openmpi_version }})

 Hello, world (8 procs total)
    --> Process #   0 of   8 is alive. -> c1
    --> Process #   4 of   8 is alive. -> c2
    --> Process #   1 of   8 is alive. -> c1
    --> Process #   5 of   8 is alive. -> c2
    --> Process #   2 of   8 is alive. -> c1
    --> Process #   6 of   8 is alive. -> c2
    --> Process #   3 of   8 is alive. -> c1
    --> Process #   7 of   8 is alive. -> c2
```

::: {.tip}

The following table provides approximate command equivalences between Flux and
SLURM:

| **Command** | **Flux** | **SLURM** |
|---|---|---|
| Submit *batch* job | flux batch [job script] | sbatch [job script] |
| Request *interactive* shell | flux alloc | salloc |
| Launch parallel tasks | flux run | srun |
| Delete job | flux cancel [job id] | scancel [job id] |
| Queue status | flux queue list | sinfo |
| Job status | flux jobs [job id] | scontrol show job [job id] |
| Node status | flux resource status | scontrol show node [node id] |
:::

## Batch execution

For batch execution, a job script with Flux directives can be submitted to the
resource manager. The example below illustrates creating such a script and
submitting a batch job for execution using the same executable referenced in
the previous interactive example.

```bash
# Create job script (and edit to set desired job sizing characteristics)
[test@sms ~]$ cat > job.mpi <<'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
#----------------------------------------------------------
# Job name
#flux: --job-name=test

# Name of stdout output file ({% raw %}{{id}}{% endraw %} expands to the job ID)
#flux: --output=job.{% raw %}{{id}}{% endraw %}.out

# Total number of nodes and task slots requested
#flux: -N 2
#flux: -n 16

# Run time - 1.5 hours
#flux: -t 90m
#----------------------------------------------------------

# Launch MPI-based executable, one MPI task per requested slot
export FLUX_JOB_NTASKS=16
prun ./a.out
EOF

# Submit job for batch execution
[test@sms ~]$ flux batch job.mpi
ƒ2kQbuWdGb5
```

::: {.tip}

The use of the `{% raw %}{{id}}{% endraw %}` token in the example batch job
script shown is a convenient way to track application output on an individual
job basis. The token is replaced with the Flux job ID once assigned, so the
output of job `ƒ2kQbuWdGb5` in this example is written to `job.f2kQbuWdGb5.out`.
The status of the job can be followed with `flux jobs`.
:::
